+        </ol>
+      </nav>
+    </aside>
+
+    <main class="chapter-main" id="main-content">
+      <header class="hero hero--fleet">
+        <span class="hero__chapter">Agentic Horizon · Lesson 12 · Tactic: Orchestrate Your Agents</span>
+        <h1>Multi-Agent Orchestration: The O-Agent</h1>
+        <p class="hero__dek">When the interface becomes the wall, put one thin custom agent in front of the fleet. Give it lifecycle tools, protect its context, and review every result through observability.</p>
+        <ul class="hero__meta" aria-label="Chapter details">
+          <li>Video spine · 56:08</li>
+          <li>Core lab · 120–180 min</li>
+          <li>Course repo · multi-agent-orchestration</li>
+          <li>11 verified 1920×1080 frames</li>
+        </ul>
+        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to explain why orchestration follows custom agents, read the O-agent through its core four, map all eight management tools to CRUD, distinguish primary agents from sub-agents, trace the observability pipeline, run the prompt-driven sleep-check loop, and conduct a plan → build → review fleet without becoming its hidden workflow engine.</p>
+
+        <div class="scale-ladder" role="img" aria-label="Agent scale ladder from base through orchestrated agents">
+          <div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>Base</b><span>Use the capable general agent.</span></div>
+          <div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>Better</b><span>Improve its context and instructions.</span></div>
+          <div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>More</b><span>Add compute through additional agents.</span></div>
+          <div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>Custom</b><span>Control the core four for a domain.</span></div>
+          <div class="scale-ladder__step scale-ladder__step--destination"><b>Orchestrated</b><span>Command the fleet through one interface.</span></div>
+        </div>
+      </header>
+
+      <div class="mobile-toc">
+        <details><summary>Chapter contents</summary><ol>
+          <li><a href="#orientation">Orientation</a></li><li><a href="#scale">The interface wall</a></li><li><a href="#o-agent">The O-agent</a></li><li><a href="#pillars">Three pillars</a></li><li><a href="#observability">Observability</a></li><li><a href="#primary">Primary agents</a></li><li><a href="#core-four">Core four at scale</a></li><li><a href="#piter">PITER out-loop</a></li><li><a href="#focus">Focus, then delete</a></li><li><a href="#results">Concrete results</a></li><li><a href="#interop">Interop and ADWs</a></li><li><a href="#gray">Operate in the gray</a></li><li><a href="#lab">Graded lab</a></li><li><a href="#mastery">Cumulative mastery</a></li><li><a href="#glossary">Glossary</a></li>
+        </ol></details>
+      </div>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
+        <span class="section-number">00</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The conductor is not the orchestra</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The dangerous first impression is that orchestration means building one enormous agent that knows everything. This lesson teaches the opposite: keep one coordinator thin, give focused primary agents protected windows, and make the system’s work observable enough to control.</p>
+        <p>You already know the prerequisites. A useful agent has a legible context, model, prompt, and tool surface. A custom agent lets you program those four. More agents add compute. But at some point, “more” becomes a human-interface problem. You open one terminal, send a prompt, wait, open another, remember what the first was doing, compare two partial outputs, and relay the useful result into a third prompt. The agents may be parallel; your attention is still serial.</p>
+        <p>The O-agent is Dan’s reference answer. It is a custom meta-agent with a completely overwritten system prompt and eight management tools exposed through an in-memory MCP server. It creates, commands, checks, interrupts, deletes, and accounts for managed agents. It does not implement the feature, inspect every log, or become a substitute builder. The orchestration app around it adds the other two pillars: CRUD-for-agents and observability.</p>
+        <aside class="callout callout--tactic"><p class="callout__label">Chapter spine</p><p><strong>One interface + CRUD-for-agents + observability</strong> turns a pile of agents into controllable compute. The app is one implementation; the three-part pattern is the transferable lesson.</p></aside>
+        <h3>What you are training</h3>
+        <ul class="learning-objectives">
+          <li><strong>Read</strong>Trace orchestrator and managed-agent behavior through the core four and the actual course files.</li>
+          <li><strong>Conduct</strong>Create, command, re-prompt, observe, interrupt, and delete agents without hopping into their terminals.</li>
+          <li><strong>Diagnose</strong>Use status tails, event filters, costs, context bars, consumed files, produced files, and diffs to locate failure.</li>
+          <li><strong>Judge</strong>Know when a fleet earns its overhead and when one focused in-loop agent is the better tool.</li>
+        </ul>
+        <figure class="frame-card">
+          <img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t0300-command-k-capability-palette.jpg" alt="Command-K palette showing the working directory, slash commands, seven agent templates, and eight orchestrator management tools" width="1920" height="1080" fetchpriority="high">
+          <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">5:00</span>The entire control surface in one frame: one prompt input, the course working directory, the ecosystem’s slash commands, seven agent templates, and all eight management tools.</figcaption>
+        </figure>
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning"><p class="callout__label">Lab prime directive</p><p>Command the fleet through the Orchestrator. Do not hand-run a managed agent in a side terminal and do not hand-edit one to rescue it. If a run fails, inspect evidence and re-command or replace the responsible agent. You are learning to conduct.</p></aside>
+        <p class="source-lock"><strong>Source lock.</strong> Every claim and lab path in this chapter comes from the AH-4 extract or the shipped <code>agentic-horizon/multi-agent-orchestration</code> repository. The Claude Agent SDK names used by this repo are already current: <code>claude-agent-sdk</code>, <code>ClaudeAgentOptions</code>, and <code>ClaudeSDKClient</code>.</p>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="scale">
+        <span class="section-number">01</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The interface becomes the wall</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Better agents improve one worker. More agents add workers. Custom agents specialize them. Orchestration arrives when creating and commanding those workers becomes the limiting step.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Ten terminals, one exhausted operator</h3><p>Picture ten capable agents, each in its own terminal. The agents can work in parallel, but you have to remember their names, purposes, last commands, current status, costs, context pressure, and outputs. You jump between windows to ask whether each is done. One result must be copied into another prompt. A failed agent looks almost the same as a slow one until you read its transcript. You added compute without adding a control plane.</p><p>Dan’s diagnosis is blunt: you cannot scale by hopping into one terminal at a time and writing one prompt at a time. When the rate at which you can create and command agents constrains engineering output, the interface—not the model—is the wall.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the scale ladder</p><h3>Each rung solves the previous rung’s constraint</h3><div class="scale-ladder"><div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>Base</b><span>General capability.</span></div><div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>Better</b><span>Higher-quality single worker.</span></div><div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>More</b><span>Parallel compute.</span></div><div class="scale-ladder__step"><b>Custom</b><span>Domain specialization.</span></div><div class="scale-ladder__step scale-ladder__step--destination"><b>Orchestrated</b><span>Fleet control through one interface.</span></div></div><p>Orchestration comes last because it depends on the earlier skills. You cannot coordinate agents well if you cannot first build a useful custom agent or observe what it is doing.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the lesson’s opening move</p><h3>From scaling compute to scaling command</h3><p>Dan starts with the agent-scale framework rather than the web application. That ordering matters. The polished UI is not the tactic; it is evidence of a tactic. The learner has already moved from base to better, then to more, then to custom. The next question is no longer “Can I make an agent?” It is “How quickly and reliably can I create, direct, inspect, and retire a fleet?”</p><p>The phrase “when your agents are slow, you’re slow” connects agent throughput to engineering throughput. But more agents only help if they pursue the right work. Ten wrong agents do not create leverage; they create ten simultaneous sources of noise.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>The agent-scale framework</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Five rungs</p><p><strong>Base → Better → More → Custom → Orchestrated.</strong> The top rung scales the human’s ability to command compute, not merely the quantity of compute available.</p></aside><p>The framework is diagnostic. If one strong agent cannot complete the job, ask whether you need better instructions, more parallelism, a custom role, or a fleet interface. Jumping straight to orchestration can hide a weak task definition behind infrastructure.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><h3>One request becomes six lifecycle actions</h3><p>The demo request asks for frontend, backend, and full-stack architecture summaries. Through Cmd+K, one prompt causes three <code>create_agent</code> calls and three <code>command_agent</code> calls. Two fast agents inspect frontend and backend; a primary agent produces the combined result. The human states the outcome once. The O-agent translates that outcome into fleet lifecycle actions.</p><p>The multiplication is real but bounded: three agents have separate model choices, contexts, costs, and event streams. The claim is not that three is always better. It is that one interface can deliberately allocate three focused windows without making the human manually relay every command.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — scale diagnosis</p><h3>Choose the missing rung</h3><ol><li>Write one engineering task you currently give an agent.</li><li>Name the present constraint: quality, throughput, specialization, or coordination.</li><li>Choose the lowest rung that solves it.</li><li>If you choose orchestration, name at least two distinct roles and the artifact each produces.</li></ol><p>Pass if your fleet choice follows from separable work. “I want it faster” is not enough; identify independent contexts or phases.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: agent count as a KPI</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you create ten agents because ten sounds powerful. Their responsibilities overlap, their outputs conflict, and no one owns synthesis.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> move down the ladder until the roles are crisp. Give each agent one purpose and a concrete result. Add orchestration only when the interface and hand-offs are the bottleneck.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>The ladder is implementation-agnostic</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>The lesson’s Vue/FastAPI/Postgres interface is bespoke. Dan explicitly says the user interface is not the payload. The durable current practice is to expose one control surface, lifecycle operations, and evidence for review—whether that surface is a web app, terminal command, SDK wrapper, or workflow trigger.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Recite the five rungs.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Base → Better → More → Custom → Orchestrated.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why does orchestration come after custom agents?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because useful orchestration presumes you can already construct specialized workers and measure their behavior.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What becomes the constraint at the top rung?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The rate and reliability with which the human can create, command, observe, and retire agents.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain in 60 seconds:</strong> “More agents” and “orchestrated agents” are not synonyms. Use the six-call demo as your proof.</p><p class="fine">Pass if you name the interface bottleneck and explain why specialization and observability are prerequisites.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="o-agent">
+        <span class="section-number">02</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">One agent to rule the interface—not do the work</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The O-agent is a custom meta-agent whose job is lifecycle management. Its value comes as much from what it refuses to do as from the tools it can call.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the overloaded conductor</p><h3>The coordinator starts implementing</h3><p>You ask an orchestrator to create a planner and a builder. It creates neither. Instead it reads the repository, drafts the plan, edits the files, runs tests, and then summarizes its own work. The result may even be correct, but your “orchestrator” is a single overloaded engineer wearing a conductor’s hat. Its context fills with implementation detail, and no independent reviewer exists.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — one interface, many workers</p><h3>Keep the routing center thin</h3><div class="fleet-architecture"><div class="fleet-architecture__node"><strong>Human</strong><p>States one goal and constraints through Cmd+K.</p></div><div class="fleet-architecture__arrow">→</div><div class="fleet-architecture__node"><strong>O-agent</strong><p>Creates, commands, checks, interrupts, reports, and deletes. It reduces and delegates.</p></div><div class="fleet-architecture__arrow">→</div><div class="fleet-architecture__node"><strong>Primary agents</strong><p>Scout, plan, build, review, and produce durable results in focused windows.</p></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the empty shell</p><h3>The UI asks for orchestration, not application input</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t0258-three-pillars-empty-shell.jpg" alt="Empty multi-agent orchestration interface with Agent Orchestration, Agent Observability, and Agents at Scale title overlays" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">4:18</span>The empty system has zero agents, zero logs, zero cost, and one invitation: ask the orchestrator to create, manage, or command agents.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan asks where the input field is because this is not a normal end-user app. The right panel is the engineer’s single interface. Everything else exists to make the fleet’s lifecycle and evidence legible.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>The single interface pattern and the O-agent</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Course definition</p><p>The <strong>Orchestrator Agent</strong> is a custom meta-agent that manages other agents. The <strong>single interface pattern</strong> places that one agent in front of the fleet.</p></aside><p>The first line of <code>backend/prompts/orchestrator_agent_system_prompt.md</code> gives the role directly. The system prompt is fully overwritten. The management surface is specialized. This is the custom-agent lesson applied one level higher.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — read its options</p><h3>The course code encodes restraint</h3><div class="code-anatomy"><div class="code-anatomy__title">apps/orchestrator_3_stream/backend/modules/orchestrator_service.py · factual shape</div><pre><code>options_dict = {
+    "system_prompt": self._load_system_prompt(),
+    "model": config.ORCHESTRATOR_MODEL,
+    "cwd": self.working_dir,
+    "resume": resume_session,
+    "env": env_vars,
+}
+
+mcp_server = create_sdk_mcp_server(
+    name="mgmt", version="1.0.0", tools=self.management_tools
+)</code></pre><div class="code-anatomy__notes"><p><b>Prompt</b> Full custom identity loaded from the orchestrator prompt file.</p><p><b>Model/context</b> Configured model, working directory, and optional resumed session.</p><p><b>Tools</b> The eight-tool <code>mgmt</code> MCP server plus Bash, Read, SlashCommand, and Skill.</p></div></div><p>The code does not make the O-agent omniscient. It gives the agent a narrow institutional role and explicit handles for delegating work.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — boundary test</p><h3>Sort actions into coordinate or execute</h3><p>Classify these actions: create a scout, read every frontend file, command a builder, inspect a ten-line status tail, edit <code>AgentList.vue</code>, ask a reviewer for PASS/FAIL, delete completed agents. The O-agent should own creation, command, bounded status, review dispatch, and deletion. Focused agents should own repository analysis and implementation.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: the cleverest agent does everything</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the O-agent performs the engineering because it has Bash and Read. Its context becomes the fleet’s dumping ground.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> enforce role language in the system prompt, produce a focused agent for the actual work, and request only compact status or artifacts. Tools provide capability; the prompt supplies purpose and restraint.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>The SDK names here are already current</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Repository truth</p><p>The backend depends on <code>claude-agent-sdk</code> and imports <code>ClaudeAgentOptions</code> and <code>ClaudeSDKClient</code>. Unlike the preceding AH-3 recording, this lesson needs no SDK-rename correction. Exact message fields can drift; the durable loop is options → mount client → query → manage response stream.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What kind of agent is the O-agent?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A custom meta-agent with a fully overwritten system prompt and specialized management tools.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What must it deliberately not do?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The heavy engineering work or exhaustive log reading.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is refusal a scaling feature?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It protects the coordinator’s context and preserves independent focused agents.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach the paradox:</strong> the O-agent becomes more useful by doing less work itself.</p><p class="fine">Pass if you connect full prompt overwrite, eight management tools, and context protection.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="pillars">
+        <span class="section-number">03</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The system stands on three pillars</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A chat box is not orchestration. A CRUD API is not orchestration. A log dashboard is not orchestration. The lesson’s system emerges only when one interface, lifecycle control, and evidence work together.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — remove one pillar</p><h3>A fleet you can start but cannot see</h3><p>Suppose the O-agent can create and command ten agents, but you cannot see what any of them read, wrote, cost, or returned. You have automation without control. Now suppose the dashboard is beautiful, but agents cannot be re-commanded or deleted. You have telemetry without lifecycle management. Finally, imagine CRUD and logs without a single interface: the human is still manually wiring every transition.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the three-pillar structure</p><h3>Each pillar answers a different control question</h3><div class="pillar-grid"><article><strong>1 · Orchestrator Agent</strong><p>Where do I state the goal? One interface translates intent into fleet actions.</p></article><article><strong>2 · CRUD-for-agents</strong><p>How do I control lifecycle? Create, read/check, update/command, interrupt, and delete.</p></article><article><strong>3 · Observability</strong><p>How do I know what happened? Events, state, cost, context, files, diffs, and results.</p></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — one prompt lights all three</p><h3>The empty shell becomes a running fleet</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t0402-populated-observability-3-agents.jpg" alt="Multi-agent observability interface populated with three agents, live event filters, model badges, costs, context bars, and orchestrator dispatch summary" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:42</span>One prompt becomes three agents. The left rail exposes model, cost, context, and event counts; the center filters live activity; the right records the orchestrator’s dispatch.</figcaption></figure><p>This frame is the system’s “aha.” The O-agent accepts the request. CRUD creates and commands the agents. Observability makes each agent’s execution and result visible. The three pillars are not architectural decoration; they are three simultaneous user capabilities.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>Multi-agent orchestration</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Course equation</p><p><strong>Orchestrator + CRUD-for-agents + Observability = controllable compute at scale.</strong></p></aside><p>“Agents at scale” means more than fan-out. Control includes changing course, deciding when an agent is done, seeing the cost of the decision, and removing the temporary resource when its purpose is complete.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the eight-tool CRUD</p><h3>Turn the acronym into actual handles</h3><div class="crud-grid"><article><strong>Create</strong><code>create_agent</code><code>command_agent</code><p>Create a worker and create or update its task.</p></article><article><strong>Read</strong><code>list_agents</code><code>check_agent_status</code><p>Read lifecycle and compact recent activity.</p></article><article><strong>Observe</strong><code>read_system_logs</code><code>report_cost</code><p>Read system evidence and orchestrator accounting.</p></article><article><strong>Delete / interrupt</strong><code>delete_agent</code><code>interrupt_agent</code><p>End a resource or stop its current execution.</p></article></div><p>CRUD is a teaching shorthand, not a perfect REST taxonomy. <code>command_agent</code> creates a task and updates the ongoing agent; <code>interrupt_agent</code> changes runtime state while also acting as a destructive lifecycle control.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — missing-pillar drill</p><h3>Diagnose three systems</h3><ol><li>A CLI launches agents but offers no status: name the missing pillar.</li><li>A dashboard displays logs but cannot re-command a worker: name the missing pillar.</li><li>An API exposes lifecycle endpoints but every call is manual: name the missing pillar.</li></ol><p>Answers: observability, CRUD/update control, and the orchestrator/single interface.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: “we have logs, so we have observability”</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> raw terminal text exists somewhere, but it cannot be filtered by agent, tied to files, or converted into a review decision.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> define evidence a conductor can act on: status, model, context, cost, response/tool/thinking/hook events, consumed files, produced diffs, and a durable final result.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Build the thinnest interface that preserves all three</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Transfer rule</p><p>You do not need this exact three-column application. A terminal orchestrator can satisfy the pattern if it remains one interface, exposes lifecycle actions, and returns enough structured evidence to review the fleet. UI polish is optional; missing control is not.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the three pillars.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Orchestrator Agent, CRUD-for-agents, and Observability.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What breaks without CRUD?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>You cannot deliberately manage the fleet’s lifecycle at scale.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What breaks without observability?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>You cannot know which agent did what, whether it worked, or whether the fleet is worth its cost.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Draw the three pillars from memory.</strong> Under each, write the user question it answers.</p><p class="fine">Pass if the answers are input/coordination, lifecycle control, and reviewable evidence.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="observability">
+        <span class="section-number">04</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Observability is the control surface</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">At fleet scale, “it looks busy” is not evidence. You need to know who acted, what they consumed, what they produced, how much it cost, and where to inspect the result.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — ten agents doing the wrong thing</p><h3>Activity masquerades as progress</h3><p>A center panel fills with events. Tool calls flash by. Token counts rise. The fleet feels productive. Yet the builder edited the wrong component, the scout read legacy code, and the reviewer verified a different path. Without per-agent evidence, more activity only makes the failure harder to locate.</p><p>Dan’s couplet is the operational standard: if you cannot measure it, you cannot improve it; if you cannot measure it, you cannot scale it.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — evidence pipeline</p><h3>From runtime event to a decision you can make</h3><div class="fleet-architecture"><div class="fleet-architecture__node"><strong>Agent runtime</strong><p>SDK messages and hooks emit response, tool, thinking, stop, and file activity.</p></div><div class="fleet-architecture__arrow">→</div><div class="fleet-architecture__node"><strong>Persistence + stream</strong><p>Postgres stores agent/system/chat records; WebSocket events update the live client.</p></div><div class="fleet-architecture__arrow">→</div><div class="fleet-architecture__node"><strong>Review surface</strong><p>Pinia-backed UI filters events, shows status/cost/context, and exposes artifacts and diffs.</p></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — consumed versus produced</p><h3>The result outlives the scrolling transcript</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t0690-produced-consumed-assets.jpg" alt="Observability interface showing consumed files, produced markdown files, additions, Show Diff controls, costs, and context for three idle agents" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:30</span>The Review end of the system: consumed source on one side, produced documents with green diff statistics and Show Diff controls on the other.</figcaption></figure><p>The view answers more than “done?” It tells you which files supplied context and which files contain the result. The agent can disappear later; the architecture document remains inspectable. One-click opening and inline diffs let the human enter the loop only at the point of judgment.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>Agent observability</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Not a cosmetic dashboard</p><p><strong>Observability</strong> is the structured evidence required to understand performance, cost, behavior, and results across the fleet.</p></aside><p>The course system tracks response, tool, thinking, and hook categories; per-agent status; tokens and cost; context; prompts; system logs; chat; and file activity. A fast model generates short event summaries so the O-agent or human can inspect a bounded tail instead of swallowing raw history.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — debug “who broke it?”</p><h3>Move from symptom to responsible agent</h3><ol><li>Filter the EventStream to the builder’s chip.</li><li>Select TOOL events and search for the target component path.</li><li>Open the produced file and its diff.</li><li>Compare consumed files with the intended scope.</li><li>Check the reviewer’s response and hook/Stop sequence.</li><li>Re-command, interrupt, or replace the agent based on evidence.</li></ol><p>This is why the central question is not “Did an agent run?” It is “Which agent made which change from which context, and what did independent review conclude?”</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — evidence sentence</p><h3>Write a falsifiable run summary</h3><p>Use this template after a lab run: “Agent <em>name</em>, using <em>model</em>, consumed <em>files</em>, produced <em>artifact/diff</em>, cost <em>amount</em>, stopped after <em>signal</em>, and passed/failed because <em>review evidence</em>.” If you cannot fill a field, identify the missing observability path.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: make the O-agent read everything</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> to stay informed, the coordinator reads complete logs for every worker. The observability pillar exists, but its use destroys coordinator context.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> use summaries, bounded tails, filters, artifacts, and status. Observability should reduce uncertainty without copying the whole fleet into one prompt.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Trust active code over stale documentation</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Course-repo accuracy</p><p>The active backend is on port <strong>9403</strong> and the frontend on <strong>5175</strong>. The inner <code>CLAUDE.md</code> reference to 8002 is stale. <code>modules/hooks.py</code> is legacy/orphan; active tracking lives in <code>command_agent_hooks.py</code> and <code>orchestrator_hooks.py</code>.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Complete the measurement couplet.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>If you cannot measure it, you cannot improve it; if you cannot measure it, you cannot scale it.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name four event filters.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Response, Tool, Thinking, and Hook.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why separate consumed from produced?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It shows the evidence an agent read and the durable result it created, making review and hand-off possible.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain to a skeptical teammate:</strong> why is observability one third of orchestration rather than an optional operations feature?</p><p class="fine">Pass if you connect measurement to control, diagnosis, cost, artifacts, and safe out-loop review.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="primary">
+        <span class="section-number">05</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Promote templates into primary agents</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The managed workers are not blind, one-shot Task calls. Each receives its own SDK client and session, can be re-commanded, and remains visible as an addressable resource until the O-agent archives it.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — “aren’t these just sub-agents?”</p><h3>The one-shot worker vanishes too early</h3><p>A scout returns a useful partial answer, but you need it to check one more path. With an ephemeral sub-agent, the narrow execution is over and its working context is gone. You can launch another scout, but you cannot address the original worker, ask a dependent follow-up, or inspect its ongoing lifecycle as a first-class resource.</p><p>That limitation is not always bad. Ephemeral sub-agents are excellent for bounded delegation. The O-agent system chooses a different primitive because orchestration needs workers that can survive across multiple commands and expose their own state.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — two worker lifecycles</p><h3>Ephemeral call versus addressable session</h3><div class="agent-contrast"><article><h3>Task sub-agent</h3><ul><li>Created for one narrow execution.</li><li>Returns a result to its parent.</li><li>Context is not an addressable fleet resource.</li><li>Useful when one bounded answer is enough.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Managed primary agent</h3><ul><li>Own <code>ClaudeSDKClient</code> and session ID.</li><li>Visible card, status, cost, events, and artifacts.</li><li>Re-commanded through <code>resume=session_id</code>.</li><li>Can run commands, skills, and its own delegations.</li></ul></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — template pulled up a level</p><h3>A reusable definition becomes a persistent worker</h3><p>The demonstration calls <code>create_agent(subagent_template="scout-report-suggest-fast")</code>. The loader resolves a file under <code>.claude/agents/</code>, uses its prompt and model to mount a fresh client, and stores the session. Later, <code>command_agent</code> resumes that session. The template came from the sub-agent ecosystem, but the runtime gives it primary-agent lifecycle.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t1445-ecosystem-interop-slash-commands.jpg" alt="Orchestrator interface showing a managed codebase-questioner agent running the question slash command and a prime command being typed" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">24:05</span>The managed <code>codebase-questioner</code> runs <code>/question</code>; the answer confirms that orchestrator and agents can use skills, while <code>/prime</code> is ready in the single input.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>Primary agent versus sub-agent</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Promotion pattern</p><p>A <strong>primary agent</strong> is an independently addressable, persistent, re-promptable SDK session. In this repo, a committed sub-agent template can be <strong>pulled up a level</strong> into that lifecycle.</p></aside><p>The distinction is about runtime and control, not the markdown file alone. The same template text can inform an ephemeral sub-agent or seed a primary agent; the session, client, observability, and command surface create the difference.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the template fleet</p><h3>Seven roles, three featured in the workflow</h3><div class="template-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Template</th><th>Declared model class</th><th>Declared job</th><th>Course use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>scout-report-suggest-fast</code></td><td>Haiku</td><td>Fast scout; frontmatter declares Read, Glob, Grep</td><td>Read and produce a scout report</td></tr><tr><td><code>scout-report-suggest</code></td><td>Sonnet</td><td>Deeper scout</td><td>More thorough repository reading</td></tr><tr><td><code>build-agent</code></td><td>Sonnet</td><td>Implement from a specification</td><td>Build phase</td></tr><tr><td><code>review-agent</code></td><td>Sonnet</td><td>Diff review and PASS/FAIL</td><td>Review phase</td></tr><tr><td><code>meta-agent</code></td><td>Opus</td><td>Design agent definitions</td><td>Agent architect</td></tr><tr><td><code>docs-scraper</code></td><td>Haiku</td><td>Collect documentation</td><td>Write into <code>ai_docs/</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>playwright-validator</code></td><td>Sonnet</td><td>Browser validation</td><td>Drive and inspect the UI</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class="callout callout--warning"><p class="callout__label">Repo-truth caveat</p><p>The fast scout template declares Read, Glob, and Grep, but the current <code>command_agent()</code> resume path rebuilds options with a broader default allowlist. Treat the frontmatter as the declared role, not a reliable runtime sandbox in this version.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — prove persistence</p><h3>Ask a dependent follow-up</h3><ol><li>Create a uniquely named agent from <code>scout-report-suggest-fast</code>.</li><li>Command it to inspect one slice of the codebase.</li><li>After the response, re-command the same name with a question that depends on the first result.</li><li>Verify the UI retains one card/session rather than creating a second worker.</li><li>Compare its declared template tools with the tools visible during resumed execution.</li></ol></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: confuse declared tools with enforced tools</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you call the fast scout “read-only” because its frontmatter lists three read tools, then resumed execution exposes broader tools.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> trace both creation and command paths. In this repository, creation applies template declarations, while the command/resume path widens the allowlist. Record the gap; do not repair it by hand during the core lab.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Use runtime evidence for capability claims</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Durable rule</p><p>Templates describe intent. Options, hooks, permissions, and the mounted runtime enforce behavior. When security or write access matters, audit the actual resumed path and test denial—not just the template’s header.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Give two abilities of a managed primary agent.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It can be re-commanded with its prior session and remains an independently observable resource; it can also run reusable commands/skills and produce durable artifacts.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What “pulls a template up a level”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Creating a fresh SDK client/session from the template and managing it as an addressable primary agent.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is the fast scout not reliably read-only here?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The current command/resume path rebuilds options with a broader default tool allowlist than the template declares.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain without saying “better”:</strong> how does a primary agent differ operationally from an ephemeral sub-agent?</p><p class="fine">Pass if you mention independent session, re-prompting, observability, and an artifact that survives the worker.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="core-four">
+        <span class="section-number">06</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Manage the core four at fleet scale</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The fleet does not introduce a new kind of intelligence. It repeats the same four controls—context, model, prompt, tools—across many agents, while protecting the one context that coordinates them.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the omniscient coordinator</p><h3>The O-agent reads every transcript</h3><p>You want the coordinator to be informed, so you feed it every scout note, tool call, reasoning block, diff, and review log. Soon the O-agent’s context contains three agents’ implementation detail plus its own orchestration history. It can no longer tell which facts are current. The single interface remains, but its perspective is polluted.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — four controls, repeated</p><h3>Every card is the same diagnostic lens</h3><div class="core-four-grid"><article><strong>Context</strong><p>Working directory, project settings, session history, and selected evidence.</p></article><article><strong>Model</strong><p>Fast class for scouts/summaries; primary class for planning, building, reviewing; high-capability class for meta-agent design.</p></article><article><strong>Prompt</strong><p>Overwritten coordinator identity, template role, dynamic planner instructions, or workflow command.</p></article><article><strong>Tools</strong><p>Management MCP for the O-agent; role-specific or current default surfaces for managed agents.</p></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — Dan names the view</p><h3>All the UI is “just the core four”</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t0528-the-core-four.jpg" alt="Core Four overlay with Context, Model, Prompt, and Tools over live multi-agent observability cards" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">8:48</span>The four-circle model sits over the live fleet. Model badges and context bars make the same reading lens visible at agent scale.</figcaption></figure><p>The interface reports context against a 200k denominator, model badge, cost, and activity. That 200k value is a course UI/reporting assumption rather than an explicit <code>ClaudeAgentOptions</code> field in this repository. The actual context mechanics include the working directory, project setting sources, prompts, tool definitions, messages, and resumed session.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>Core four at scale + R&amp;D</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Coordinator discipline</p><p><strong>Reduce</strong> what the O-agent must hold. <strong>Delegate</strong> focused work to agents with their own windows. The R&amp;D framework keeps the conductor thinner than the orchestra’s combined work.</p></aside><p>The orchestrator prompt sets a compact threshold of 80 percent and can suggest commanding an agent with <code>/compact</code>. More important than the number is the design: the coordinator asks for status and results rather than importing all raw work.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — match model to job</p><h3>Allocate capability across the fleet</h3><div class="cost-board"><article><h3>High-volume, narrow reading</h3><p>The fast scouts, documentation collector, and event summaries use the Haiku-class model in the course configuration. Their work is scoped, repeated, and mostly read/summarize shaped.</p></article><article><h3>Primary engineering and judgment</h3><p>The O-agent, planner, builder, reviewer, and primary worker use the Sonnet-class model. The meta-agent template uses Opus for agent architecture. Exact pinned IDs are period detail; the lesson is task-to-model matching.</p></article></div><p>The code’s source of truth is <code>config.py</code>: <code>DEFAULT_MODEL</code> and <code>FAST_MODEL</code>. The orchestrator system prompt still contains an older Haiku documentation line. Trust active resolution code and on-screen badges over the stale prompt note.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — core-four worksheet</p><h3>Read two agents side by side</h3><p>For the O-agent and one managed scout, record: context inputs and session continuity; model source; prompt source; tools declared and tools actually mounted. Circle every difference. Then write one sentence explaining why the O-agent should know less implementation detail than the scout.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: context percentage as the whole story</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> a low context bar is treated as proof of clean context, or a high bar as automatic failure.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> inspect composition and role. The O-agent should carry compact status and decisions; a planner may legitimately consume many source files. Context quality is relevance and isolation, not only a percentage.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Model IDs drift; allocation principles last</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Do not memorize the pin</p><p>The repository pins Sonnet 4.5 and Haiku 4.5 strings and declares Opus for the meta-agent. Keep those as source-reading details. Re-check available models when operating the code; preserve the principle of inexpensive narrow scouts and stronger models for synthesis, implementation, review, and agent design.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the core four.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt, and tools.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which two R&amp;D verbs protect the O-agent?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Reduce and Delegate.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Where does the displayed 200k value come from?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The course UI/reporting code visualizes usage against 200k; this repo does not pass a 200k option in <code>ClaudeAgentOptions</code>.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach the fleet through four questions:</strong> what does each agent know, which model works, what is its law, and what can it do?</p><p class="fine">Pass if your coordinator answer includes deliberate ignorance through Reduce and Delegate.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="piter">
+        <span class="section-number">07</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">PITER keeps you out of the loop without abandoning review</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Out-loop work is not hope-driven autonomy. Prompt input enters through a trigger, agents operate inside a bounded environment, and observability gives the human a review surface.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — unattended or unreviewed?</p><h3>The false choice between babysitting and blind trust</h3><p>Some engineers stay glued to every token because they equate absence with risk. Others launch a fleet and walk away because they equate autonomy with maturity. Both miss the system. Staying out of the loop means the human does not manually carry every transition. It does not remove acceptance criteria, evidence, or a final judgment.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the four-stage out-loop map</p><h3>Input, trigger, environment, review</h3><div class="piter-map"><div class="piter-map__step"><b>P</b><strong>Prompt input</strong><span>Cmd+K message stating goal and constraints.</span></div><div class="piter-map__step"><b>T</b><strong>Trigger</strong><span><code>POST /send_chat</code> reaches FastAPI and background execution.</span></div><div class="piter-map__step"><b>E</b><strong>Environment</strong><span>The configured working directory and project setting sources.</span></div><div class="piter-map__step"><b>R</b><strong>Review</strong><span>WebSocket/DB-backed events, status, files, diffs, and reports.</span></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the system builds itself</p><h3>A prompt crosses real application boundaries</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t0636-architecture-mermaid.jpg" alt="Light Mermaid architecture diagram tracing frontend HTTP and WebSocket through FastAPI, Orchestrator Service, Claude SDK, O-agent, management MCP, and Agent Manager" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">10:36</span>An agent-generated architecture map of the system it just inspected: browser → FastAPI → Orchestrator Service → O-agent → management MCP → Agent Manager, with WebSocket streaming back.</figcaption></figure><p>The application targets its own <code>apps/orchestrator_3_stream</code> directory, so the demo literally uses the system to build the system. The prompt enters through HTTP, background work runs against the configured directory, and the result returns through persistence and the live event stream.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>PITER, tactic four</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Transcript correction</p><p><strong>PITER</strong> means Prompt input → Trigger → Environment → Review. The automatic transcript writes “Peter.” PITER is a lesson concept mapped onto the repo; it is not a string to search for in source.</p></aside><p>The associated tactic is Stay Out the Loop. “Review” keeps the phrase honest: the human changes position from continuous relay operator to evaluator at meaningful checkpoints.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — map one request</p><h3>Architecture summary through PITER</h3><div class="result-chain"><article><h3>Out-loop execution</h3><p><strong>P:</strong> request frontend/backend/full-stack summaries.</p><p><strong>T:</strong> one chat request launches O-agent execution.</p><p><strong>E:</strong> three primary agents read the course app directory.</p></article><article><h3>Human review</h3><p><strong>R:</strong> inspect each agent’s status, consumed sources, produced architecture markdown, cost, and diff. Enter the file only when judgment is useful.</p><p>The system carries transitions; the human still decides whether the architecture account is correct.</p></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — map your environment</p><h3>Make every PITER stage concrete</h3><p>Choose one orchestration-shaped task. Write the exact input, the event or endpoint that triggers it, the directory and permissions that define its environment, and the evidence required for review. If your R stage says only “read the answer,” add a diff, test, artifact, cost, or independent reviewer.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: out of the loop becomes out of control</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the trigger launches work, but the environment is broad and the review criterion is “looks okay.”</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> bind the working directory, specify artifacts, record consumed and produced files, and require a reviewer or deterministic check. Review is designed before launch, not improvised after surprise.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>This reference implementation is one PITER mapping</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Transfer without copying the stack</p><p>FastAPI, asyncio, Postgres, WebSockets, and Pinia are the course implementation. A cron trigger, issue event, CLI invocation, or job queue can occupy T; a worktree or container can define E; CI and a review artifact can define R. Preserve the four responsibilities.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Expand PITER.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompt input → Trigger → Environment → Review.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which tactic does it operationalize?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tactic four: Stay Out the Loop.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is “Peter” absent from the repo?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is a transcript artifact for the conceptual acronym PITER, not a code identifier.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach PITER using the course app.</strong> Do not use generic definitions; name the request, endpoint, directory, and review evidence.</p><p class="fine">Pass if Review is concrete enough to reject a bad result.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="focus">
+        <span class="section-number">08</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">One agent, one prompt, one purpose—then archive it</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The fleet is a pool of temporary focused resources, not a collection of immortal coworkers. Preserve the result, retire the worker, and use a fresh name and window for the next purpose.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — context hoarding</p><h3>The agent that never goes home</h3><p>A useful scout becomes a planner, then a builder, then a reviewer. Each new task inherits irrelevant source, old assumptions, and earlier instructions. You keep it because the session feels valuable, but its accumulated context makes it harder to predict. The 200k display looks spacious until the worker carries five jobs and cannot tell which role governs the next command.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — disposable worker, durable work</p><h3>Separate resource lifetime from artifact lifetime</h3><div class="result-chain"><article><h3>Temporary agent</h3><p>One protected context, one prompt, one purpose, one lifecycle. Create → command → inspect → archive.</p></article><article><h3>Persistent result</h3><p>Scout report, plan, implementation diff, review report, logs, and cost evidence remain available after the agent leaves the visible fleet.</p></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — delete all agents</p><h3>The empty rail is a successful end state</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t1143-crud-delete-all-agents.jpg" alt="O-agent interface showing three delete_agent actions followed by all agents deleted successfully" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">19:03</span>Three <code>delete_agent</code> actions clear the visible fleet. The produced documentation remains; the temporary compute has finished its purpose.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan connects deletion to engineering maturity: eventually you learn that the best code can be no code. Agents deserve the same discipline. Retaining a polluted session because it once helped is not thrift; it is deferred confusion.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>One agent, one prompt, one purpose</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Tactic six carried forward</p><p>Treat agents as <strong>deletable temporary resources</strong> serving one purpose. The work product persists; the worker does not need to.</p></aside><p>In the course database, “delete” is implemented as a soft archive: <code>archived = true</code>. It removes the agent from the active fleet but does not physically delete the row. That distinction matters operationally.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — lifecycle with a suffix</p><h3>Create, re-command, retire, never recycle the name</h3><ol><li>Create <code>backend-scout-k7</code> for one architecture report.</li><li>Re-command it only to clarify that same report.</li><li>Confirm the artifact exists under <code>app_docs/</code>.</li><li>Ask the O-agent to delete it; observe that it disappears from active cards.</li><li>For the next run, use <code>backend-scout-m2</code>, not the archived name.</li></ol><p>The migrations enforce uniqueness on <code>(orchestrator_agent_id, name)</code> even for archived rows. A deleted name remains reserved. Unique suffixes turn that repo constraint into predictable lab hygiene.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — define the purpose boundary</p><h3>Write the deletion condition before launch</h3><p>For one planned agent, complete: “This agent exists to produce ____ from ____. I may re-command it only to clarify ____. I will archive it when ____ proves complete.” A good answer names a file or review verdict, not a feeling.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: reuse an archived name</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> creation fails even though the old agent no longer appears in the rail.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> recognize soft archive plus the non-partial uniqueness constraint. Generate a fresh suffix. Do not confuse absence from the active UI with physical deletion from Postgres.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Lifecycle semantics belong in the contract</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Implementation honesty</p><p>“Delete,” “archive,” “interrupt,” and “terminate” are not interchangeable. In production, document whether state, logs, costs, artifacts, and names remain. This lesson’s UI says delete while the repository archives the database row.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">State the three-part focus rule.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>One agent, one prompt, one purpose.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What survives retirement?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Produced artifacts, database/log evidence, and costs; the agent leaves the active fleet.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why can’t the same name be reused?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Delete is a soft archive, and the database uniqueness constraint still includes archived rows.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain why deleting an agent can increase reliability.</strong> Include context pollution, artifact persistence, and the repository’s archive semantics.</p><p class="fine">Pass if you do not claim the database row is physically removed.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="results">
+        <span class="section-number">09</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Every agent produces a concrete result</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The artifact is the hand-off boundary between temporary compute and durable engineering. It makes out-loop review possible and reveals whether your own specialization is actually encoded.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — the impressive conversation</p><h3>The scout “understands” the codebase but leaves nothing</h3><p>An agent reads hundreds of lines, explains its thinking in the event stream, and finishes with “I have a good understanding.” The run consumed time and tokens, but the builder receives no structured report, the human has nothing stable to review, and deletion would erase the only convenient handle on the insight. Activity without a result is not a hand-off.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — knowledge becomes an asset</p><h3>Consumed evidence → specialized work → produced artifact</h3><div class="result-chain"><article><h3>Agent lifetime</h3><p>Read bounded source, follow a role-specific prompt, use the appropriate model and tools, and record observable execution.</p></article><article><h3>Artifact lifetime</h3><p>Write a scout report, plan, code diff, architecture document, or review verdict in the structure the next consumer expects.</p></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the scout report lands</p><h3>A focused agent hands work to the next phase</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t2148-sleep-check-agent-status.jpg" alt="Scout and build agents with a completed scout report, consumed files, produced markdown artifact, and O-agent check_agent_status action" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">35:48</span>The scout’s result is explicit: a completed report, its consumed Vue/CSS sources, and a produced <code>app_docs/header-pills-scout-report.md</code>. The O-agent checks status while the builder waits.</figcaption></figure><p>The report follows a dedicated structure because the engineering has been templated into the scout’s prompt. The artifact is not incidental output; it is the contract between the scout phase and the builder phase.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>Results-oriented engineering and templating your engineering</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">The durable edge</p><p><strong>Results-oriented engineering</strong> requires each agent to leave a concrete inspectable outcome. <strong>Templating your engineering</strong> encodes your preferred structure, checks, and hand-offs in agents and workflows.</p></aside><p>The app shell is reproducible. Your moat is the specialized engineering loaded into prompts, templates, tools, report formats, and review criteria. If an out-of-the-box prompt can reproduce the entire advantage with no domain investment, the advantage is not very defensible.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — a defensible report contract</p><h3>Make “summarize the backend” operational</h3><div class="code-anatomy"><div class="code-anatomy__title">Suggested contract using the course’s existing report pattern</div><pre><code>SCOUT REPORT
+- Scope: backend orchestration lifecycle
+- Sources consumed: exact repository paths
+- Core-four findings: context, model, prompt, tools
+- Lifecycle findings: create, command, status, archive
+- Risks/gaps: evidence with paths
+- Handoff: what the builder or reviewer should do next
+- Output: app_docs/&lt;task&gt;-scout-report.md</code></pre><div class="code-anatomy__notes"><p><b>Bounded</b> The scope prevents “understand the whole repo.”</p><p><b>Traceable</b> Exact consumed paths let a reviewer audit claims.</p><p><b>Composable</b> The next role receives a predictable artifact.</p></div></div><p>This structure does not invent a new application feature; it applies the course’s demonstrated dedicated scout-report idea to the same course repository.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — artifact-first prompt</p><h3>Specify the result before the work</h3><p>Rewrite “look at the filtering code” as an artifact contract. Name the exact output path, the source paths or bounded search surface, required sections, and the consumer. Your agent prompt is complete only when another agent could use the result without reading the transcript.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: output exists but is not a hand-off</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the agent writes a long markdown diary with no source paths, decision, or next step.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> template the result around its consumer. A builder needs constraints and target files; a reviewer needs acceptance criteria and changed paths; a human needs evidence and a verdict.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Artifacts make heterogeneous tools interoperable</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Session APIs and agent interfaces change. A repository-local plan, report, diff, or review record remains legible to humans, future agents, CI, and alternate orchestration surfaces. Durable files lower coupling between ephemeral runtimes.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why must every agent produce a result?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>So its work is inspectable, hand-off ready, and durable after the temporary agent is archived.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Where does defensibility come from?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The specialized engineering encoded in prompts, tools, templates, workflows, structures, and review criteria—not the generic app shell.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What makes a report composable?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A bounded scope, traceable evidence, predictable structure, and a named next consumer.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain the statement:</strong> “The agent is temporary; the engineering is in the artifact and template.”</p><p class="fine">Pass if you connect artifact lifetime, hand-offs, review, and specialization.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="interop">
+        <span class="section-number">10</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The O-agent is glue for the existing ecosystem</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Templates, slash commands, skills, hooks, and the Agent SDK do not disappear at fleet scale. The O-agent composes them into orchestration prompts that create and monitor multi-phase work.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — rebuild everything for orchestration</p><h3>A new control plane becomes a new island</h3><p>You invest in custom agents, commands, skills, and hooks. Then you build an orchestrator that can only send raw prompts to anonymous workers. Your best engineering assets sit outside the fleet, and every workflow must be reimplemented inside the app. The control plane added scale by discarding reuse.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — existing assets plug into the fleet</p><h3>One coordinator, many reusable surfaces</h3><div class="pillar-grid"><article><strong>Agent templates</strong><p>Seed scout, builder, reviewer, browser validator, docs, and meta-agent roles.</p></article><article><strong>Commands + skills</strong><p>Managed agents can run reusable <code>/question</code>, <code>/prime</code>, and model-invocable workflows.</p></article><article><strong>Hooks + streams</strong><p>Observe lifecycle and file activity; apply the same governance and event patterns.</p></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — plan, build, review</p><h3>Three primary agents appear up front</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t2625-plan-build-review-workflow.jpg" alt="Plan-build-review workflow showing a dynamic planner, build-agent builder, review-agent reviewer, consumed Vue files, and produced responsive UI plan" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">43:45</span>The ADW-shaped workflow: a dynamic planner with no template badge, a <code>build-agent</code> builder, and a <code>review-agent</code> reviewer. The plan is a durable file under <code>specs/</code>.</figcaption></figure><p>The O-agent does not pass an opaque hidden context from one worker to the next. It creates the roles, commands the appropriate phase, waits, checks completion evidence, and uses produced artifacts to connect the work. That is why Dan calls it glue.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>Ecosystem interoperability, orchestration prompts, and ADWs</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Workflow composition</p><p>An <strong>orchestration prompt</strong> tells the O-agent how to create, command, monitor, and retire a multi-agent sequence. The demonstrated <strong>ADW</strong> shape is plan → build → review → report.</p></aside><p>The planner is dynamic—its prompt is built for the task and it has no committed agent template. The builder and reviewer use reusable templates. This contrast shows that a workflow can combine static institutional roles with task-specific roles.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — prompt-driven sleep-check</p><h3>The monitoring loop lives in markdown</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t3215-reviewer-verify-finale.jpg" alt="Reviewer agent running a Vite build while the O-agent executes Bash sleep 20 and then check_agent_status" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">53:35</span>The reviewer verifies the Vite build while the coordinator visibly runs <code>Bash sleep 20</code> and prepares another <code>check_agent_status</code> call.</figcaption></figure><div class="code-anatomy"><div class="code-anatomy__title">.claude/commands/orch_plan_w_scouts_build_review.md · behavioral loop</div><pre><code>SLEEP_INTERVAL: 15 seconds
+
+repeat:
+  Bash(sleep 15)
+  check_agent_status(agent_name, tail_count, ...)
+until:
+  response event_category
+  followed by hook Stop</code></pre><div class="code-anatomy__notes"><p><b>Prompt-driven</b> The loop is orchestration behavior, not hidden scheduler code.</p><p><b>Bounded evidence</b> Status tails protect coordinator context.</p><p><b>Completion</b> Response followed by Stop closes the observed phase.</p></div></div><p>The frame uses a 20-second sleep in the live run; the command declares a 15-second interval. The lesson is not the exact duration. It is that readable orchestration instructions supply the glue.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — trace the workflow</p><h3>Open the prompt before running it</h3><ol><li>Find the planner, builder, and reviewer creation instructions.</li><li>Locate <code>SLEEP_INTERVAL</code>, <code>Bash(sleep)</code>, and <code>check_agent_status</code>.</li><li>Find the response-then-Stop completion condition.</li><li>Name every produced artifact that crosses a phase boundary.</li><li>Predict what the O-agent will do if the reviewer remains executing.</li></ol></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: assume the monitor is hidden machinery</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you search Python for a 15-second polling service, cannot find it, and decide the behavior is magical.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> read the workflow command. The O-agent follows prompt instructions to sleep and call its own status tool. Change the orchestration behavior where it is encoded.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Preserve inspectable orchestration</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Optional source-approved supplement</p><p>The course’s SDK and ecosystem names are current, while exact APIs keep evolving. For the wider programmable engineering surface, the extract-approved video <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6fCqj4xFCZI">How Claude Code CHANGED Engineering Forever (and what’s next)</a> remains a supplemental bridge; the chapter itself relies only on the course source.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the three main phases.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Plan → Build → Review, followed by a report.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which roles use templates?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Builder uses <code>build-agent</code> and reviewer uses <code>review-agent</code>; the planner is dynamic with no static template.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">How does the monitor loop work?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The workflow prompt directs Bash sleep followed by <code>check_agent_status</code> until response and Stop evidence indicate completion.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain why the O-agent is “glue.”</strong> Use one static template, the dynamic planner, one artifact, and the sleep-check loop.</p><p class="fine">Pass if the coordinator connects phases without doing their engineering work.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="gray">
+        <span class="section-number">11</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Build the agentic layer honestly—in the gray</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The O-agent is both part of the agentic layer and a tool for discovering how that layer should be organized. It offers leverage now, while carrying real infrastructure cost and acknowledged missing pieces.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — wait for the finished future</p><h3>The perfect out-loop system never arrives</h3><p>You postpone orchestration until it has deterministic workflows, human approval gates, context forking, multi-codebase control, prompt autocomplete, flawless agent management, and zero maintenance. The requirements describe a mature platform, so no experiment survives long enough to teach you which parts your codebase actually needs.</p><p>The opposite mistake is equally damaging: present a custom demo as a universal finished product. Dan refuses both extremes. The system is useful, expensive, incomplete, and worth learning from.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — value versus carrying cost</p><h3>The O-agent lives inside a larger investment decision</h3><div class="cost-board"><article><h3>Leverage gained</h3><ul><li>One interface for a fleet.</li><li>Reusable custom roles and workflows.</li><li>Out-loop review with evidence.</li><li>A practical way to organize the agentic layer.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Cost accepted</h3><ul><li>Orchestrator and worker token spend.</li><li>Postgres, backend, WebSocket, and UI maintenance.</li><li>Prompt and template drift.</li><li>Incomplete human and deterministic integration.</li></ul></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the chapter’s honest ending</p><h3>Prioritize agentics, then name the missing piece</h3><p>Dan gives a rough guideline: spend more than 50 percent of your time investing in the agentic layer—the system that builds the system. In the same closing sequence, he names the biggest missing piece: broader integration with deterministic AI Developer Workflows. The lesson’s plan/build/review orchestration prompt is ADW-shaped, but the complete deterministic layer is future work.</p><p>Other absent features are explicit: human-in-the-loop decision points, better management across multiple codebases, context forking, and prompt autocomplete. These belong in a “not built yet” ledger, not a feature list.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>Prioritize agentics and operate in the gray</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Two simultaneous truths</p><p>The O-agent is a meaningful part of the <strong>agentic layer</strong>, and it is not the completed layer. <strong>Operating in the gray</strong> means investing with evidence while acknowledging maintenance and missing edges.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — orchestrate or stay single</p><h3>Use task shape, not ambition</h3><div class="agent-contrast"><article><h3>Choose a fleet</h3><p>A repeated change requires independent repository scouting, a durable plan, implementation, and a separate PASS/FAIL review. Each phase has a clean artifact, parallelizable context, and enough recurrence to repay orchestration.</p></article><article><h3>Choose one in-loop agent</h3><p>You are exploring an ambiguous UI idea and expect rapid back-and-forth. Roles would overlap, the target changes every few minutes, and the infrastructure would cost more attention than it removes.</p></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — judgment ledger</p><h3>Write both sides before building</h3><p>For one real task, list: separable roles, expected artifacts, recurrence, review needs, token/runtime cost, infrastructure dependencies, and what remains human. Decide fleet or single agent. A valid “no” is expert behavior when the work is exploratory, generic, or tightly interactive.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: confuse “more than 50%” with a universal budget</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the rough guideline becomes an accounting mandate, detached from team maturity or system value.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> keep it as Dan’s prioritization heuristic: invest substantially in the system that builds the system. Track whether that investment removes recurring coordination, improves evidence, and increases reliable throughput.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Publish the “not yet” ledger beside the architecture</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Explicitly not present in this repo</p><p>Do not claim built-in human decision gates, multi-codebase orchestration through one UI, context-window forking, or prompt autocomplete. Do not claim complete deterministic ADW integration. These are named future directions in the lesson.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the rough time-investment guideline?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>More than 50 percent on the agentic layer, presented as a rough guideline.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the biggest named missing piece?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Broader integration with deterministic AI Developer Workflows.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name two other missing features.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Human decision points, multi-codebase management, context forking, or prompt autocomplete.</p></details></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Give a two-minute investment pitch with a dissent paragraph.</strong> Explain why the O-agent is worth building, then name the costs and features it does not have.</p><p class="fine">Pass if your pitch would let a teammate choose “not yet” without misunderstanding the lesson.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <!-- CONTINUED -->
+    </main>
+  </div>
+</body>
+</html>

diff --git a/IndyDevDan/book/vol-2-ah/12-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent.assets.css b/IndyDevDan/book/vol-2-ah/12-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent.assets.css
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..94e79d76b805aeb6cf484764170abca36364fdc9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/IndyDevDan/book/vol-2-ah/12-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent.assets.css
@@ -0,0 +1,338 @@
+/* AH-4 inherits the TAC-1 gold teaching grammar and adds fleet-system visuals. */
+@import url("../vol-1-tac/01-hello-agentic-coding.assets.css");
+
+:root {
+  --chapter-wide: 68.75rem;
+}
+
+body {
+  background:
+    radial-gradient(circle at 88% 2%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-wash) 74%, transparent) 0 18rem, transparent 40rem),
+    linear-gradient(180deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 94%, var(--bg-card)) 0, var(--bg) 36rem);
+}
+
+.skip-link {
+  position: fixed;
+  top: .5rem;
+  left: .5rem;
+  z-index: 120;
+  padding: .65rem .85rem;
+  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
+  color: var(--ink-inverse);
+  background: var(--accent-ink);
+  transform: translateY(-180%);
+}
+
+.skip-link:focus { transform: none; }
+
+.hero--fleet {
+  background:
+    linear-gradient(128deg, transparent 0 56%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-wash) 88%, transparent) 56% 100%),
+    var(--bg-card);
+}
+
+.hero--fleet::after { display: none; }
+
+.scale-ladder,
+.pillar-grid,
+.fleet-architecture,
+.piter-map,
+.core-four-grid,
+.crud-grid,
+.agent-contrast,
+.result-chain,
+.workflow-map,
+.practice-grid,
+.failure-grid,
+.mastery-grid,
+.lab-evidence,
+.tool-table,
+.template-table,
+.code-anatomy,
+.cost-board {
+  width: 100%;
+  max-width: min(100%, var(--chapter-wide));
+  margin-inline: auto;
+}
+
+.scale-ladder {
+  position: relative;
+  z-index: 1;
+  display: grid;
+  grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));
+  gap: var(--space-2);
+  margin-top: var(--space-6);
+}
+
+.scale-ladder__step {
+  position: relative;
+  min-height: 8.5rem;
+  padding: var(--space-4);
+  border: var(--hairline);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-card) 86%, var(--accent-wash));
+  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
+}
+
+.scale-ladder__step:not(:last-child)::after {
+  position: absolute;
+  top: 50%;
+  right: -.72rem;
+  z-index: 2;
+  display: grid;
+  place-items: center;
+  width: 1.35rem;
+  height: 1.35rem;
+  border-radius: 50%;
+  color: var(--ink-inverse);
+  background: var(--accent);
+  content: "→";
+  font-weight: 800;
+  transform: translateY(-50%);
+}
+
+.scale-ladder__step b { display: block; color: var(--accent-ink); font: 700 var(--text-md)/1 var(--font-display); }
+.scale-ladder__step span { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-2); color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
+.scale-ladder__step--destination { border: 2px solid var(--accent); background: var(--accent-wash); }
+
+.pillar-grid,
+.core-four-grid,
+.crud-grid,
+.practice-grid,
+.failure-grid,
+.mastery-grid,
+.lab-evidence {
+  display: grid;
+  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
+  gap: var(--space-3);
+}
+
+.pillar-grid article,
+.core-four-grid article,
+.crud-grid article,
+.practice-grid article,
+.failure-grid article,
+.mastery-grid article,
+.lab-evidence article {
+  padding: var(--space-5);
+  border: var(--hairline);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  background: var(--bg-card);
+  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
+}
+
+.pillar-grid article { border-top: .4rem solid var(--accent); }
+.pillar-grid strong,
+.core-four-grid strong,
+.crud-grid strong,
+.practice-grid strong,
+.failure-grid strong,
+.mastery-grid strong,
+.lab-evidence strong { display: block; color: var(--accent-ink); font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--text-md); }
+.pillar-grid p,
+.core-four-grid p,
+.crud-grid p,
+.practice-grid p,
+.failure-grid p,
+.mastery-grid p,
+.lab-evidence p { margin-top: var(--space-2); color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
+.failure-grid article { border-top: 4px solid var(--warn); }
+
+.fleet-architecture {
+  display: grid;
+  grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1.25fr auto 1fr;
+  gap: var(--space-3);
+  align-items: stretch;
+  padding: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 3rem);
+  border: 1px solid var(--accent);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bg-card), var(--accent-wash));
+}
+
+.fleet-architecture__node {
+  padding: var(--space-5);
+  border: var(--hairline);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  background: var(--bg-card);
+}
+
+.fleet-architecture__node strong { display: block; color: var(--accent-ink); font: 700 var(--text-md) var(--font-display); }
+.fleet-architecture__node p { margin-top: var(--space-3); color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
+.fleet-architecture__arrow { display: grid; place-items: center; color: var(--accent); font: 800 var(--text-xl) var(--font-display); }
+
+.piter-map {
+  display: grid;
+  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
+  gap: var(--space-3);
+  padding: var(--space-5);
+  border: var(--hairline);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  background: var(--bg-card);
+  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
+}
+
+.piter-map__step {
+  position: relative;
+  min-height: 11rem;
+  padding: var(--space-5);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
+  background: var(--bg-inset);
+}
+
+.piter-map__step:not(:last-child)::after { position: absolute; top: 50%; right: -.9rem; z-index: 2; color: var(--accent); content: "→"; font-size: var(--text-lg); font-weight: 800; }
+.piter-map__step b { display: block; color: var(--accent-ink); font: 800 var(--text-xl)/1 var(--font-display); }
+.piter-map__step strong { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-2); }
+.piter-map__step span { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-2); color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
+
+.core-four-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
+.core-four-grid article { min-height: 12rem; border-top: .35rem solid var(--accent); }
+
+.crud-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
+.crud-grid article { border-top: .35rem solid var(--ok); }
+.crud-grid code { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-2); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
+
+.agent-contrast,
+.result-chain,
+.workflow-map,
+.cost-board {
+  display: grid;
+  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
+  overflow: hidden;
+  border: 1px solid var(--accent);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  background: var(--bg-card);
+  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
+}
+
+.agent-contrast article,
+.result-chain article,
+.workflow-map article,
+.cost-board article { padding: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2.7rem); }
+.agent-contrast article:first-child,
+.result-chain article:first-child,
+.workflow-map article:first-child,
+.cost-board article:first-child { background: var(--accent-wash); }
+.agent-contrast article + article,
+.result-chain article + article,
+.workflow-map article + article,
+.cost-board article + article { border-left: var(--hairline); }
+.agent-contrast h3,
+.result-chain h3,
+.workflow-map h3,
+.cost-board h3 { margin: 0; }
+
+.workflow-map { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
+.workflow-map article:nth-child(2) { background: var(--bg-card); }
+.workflow-map article:nth-child(3) { background: var(--ok-wash); }
+
+.tool-table,
+.template-table {
+  overflow-x: auto;
+  border: var(--hairline);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  background: var(--bg-card);
+  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
+}
+
+.tool-table table,
+.template-table table { min-width: 62rem; margin: 0; }
+.tool-table tbody tr { border-left: .35rem solid var(--accent); }
+.template-table tbody tr { border-left: .35rem solid var(--ok); }
+
+.code-anatomy {
+  overflow: hidden;
+  border: 1px solid var(--terminal-border);
+  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
+  color: var(--terminal-ink);
+  background: var(--terminal-bg);
+  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
+}
+
+.code-anatomy__title { padding: .75rem 1rem; color: var(--terminal-ink); background: var(--terminal-bar); font: 700 var(--text-sm) var(--font-mono); }
+.code-anatomy pre { margin: 0; border: 0; border-radius: 0; color: inherit; background: transparent; }
+.code-anatomy__notes { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1px; color: var(--ink); background: var(--terminal-border); }
+.code-anatomy__notes p { padding: var(--space-3); background: var(--bg-card); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
+.code-anatomy__notes b { color: var(--accent-ink); }
+
+.source-lock {
+  padding: var(--space-4);
+  border-left: 4px solid var(--ink-mute);
+  color: var(--ink-soft);
+  background: var(--bg-inset);
+  font-size: var(--text-sm);
+}
+
+.lab-checklist { padding: 0; list-style: none; }
+.lab-checklist li { position: relative; padding-left: 2rem; }
+.lab-checklist li::before { position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--accent-ink); content: "□"; font: 700 var(--text-md) var(--font-mono); }
+
+.chapter-main .frame-card {
+  width: 100%;
+  max-width: min(100%, 68.75rem);
+  margin-inline: auto;
+}
+
+.chapter-main .frame-card img {
+  display: block;
+  width: 100%;
+  max-width: 68.75rem;
+  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
+  object-fit: contain;
+  background: var(--bg);
+}
+
+.frame-card figcaption { max-width: none; }
+.frame-card__t { display: inline-flex; margin-right: .5rem; padding: .12rem .42rem; border-radius: 999px; color: var(--accent-ink); background: var(--accent-wash); font: 750 var(--text-xs) var(--font-mono); }
+
+@media (max-width: 72rem) {
+  .scale-ladder { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
+  .scale-ladder__step::after { display: none !important; }
+  .core-four-grid,
+  .crud-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
+  .fleet-architecture { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
+  .fleet-architecture__arrow { transform: rotate(90deg); }
+}
+
+@media (max-width: 52rem) {
+  .scale-ladder,
+  .pillar-grid,
+  .core-four-grid,
+  .crud-grid,
+  .practice-grid,
+  .failure-grid,
+  .mastery-grid,
+  .lab-evidence,
+  .piter-map,
+  .agent-contrast,
+  .result-chain,
+  .workflow-map,
+  .cost-board { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
+  .piter-map__step::after { display: none !important; }
+  .agent-contrast article + article,
+  .result-chain article + article,
+  .workflow-map article + article,
+  .cost-board article + article { border-top: var(--hairline); border-left: 0; }
+  .code-anatomy__notes { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
+}
+
+@media print {
+  .scale-ladder,
+  .pillar-grid,
+  .fleet-architecture,
+  .piter-map,
+  .core-four-grid,
+  .crud-grid,
+  .agent-contrast,
+  .result-chain,
+  .workflow-map,
+  .code-anatomy,
+  .tool-table,
+  .template-table,
+  .frame-card { break-inside: avoid; }
+  .frame-card img { max-height: 4.25in; object-fit: contain; }
+  .pillar-grid,
+  .practice-grid,
+  .failure-grid,
+  .mastery-grid,
+  .lab-evidence { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
+}
