   485	          <p class="fine">Expert pass: your listener can open the course repo, locate the eight-tool management server, predict which artifacts and events should appear, and explain the difference between the lesson ideal and the current implementation gaps.</p>
   486	        </div>
   487	      </section>
   488	
   489	      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
   490	        <span class="section-number">14</span>
   491	        <h2 class="section-title">AH-4 glossary</h2>
   492	        <p class="section-lead">Use the terms as operating handles. Each definition points to a visible behavior or real course-code surface.</p>
   493	        <dl class="glossary">
   494	          <div><dt>Agent-scale framework</dt><dd>Base → Better → More → Custom → Orchestrated; a diagnostic ladder for locating the current constraint.</dd></div>
   495	          <div><dt>O-agent / OA / Orchestrator Agent</dt><dd>The custom meta-agent that manages the fleet through a fully overwritten system prompt and eight management tools.</dd></div>
   496	          <div><dt>Single interface pattern</dt><dd>One interface in front of many resources; here, one agent turns an engineer’s request into fleet lifecycle actions.</dd></div>
   497	          <div><dt>Three pillars</dt><dd>Orchestrator Agent, CRUD-for-agents, and Observability.</dd></div>
   498	          <div><dt>CRUD-for-agents</dt><dd>The create, list/check, command/update, interrupt, report, and archive controls that make managed-agent lifecycle addressable.</dd></div>
   499	          <div><dt>Management MCP server</dt><dd>The in-memory server named <code>mgmt</code> that exposes the eight management tools to the O-agent.</dd></div>
   500	          <div><dt>Primary agent</dt><dd>A managed worker with its own SDK client/session, observable lifecycle, re-commandable context, and durable results.</dd></div>
   501	          <div><dt>Sub-agent</dt><dd>An ephemeral, narrow delegation that returns a result to its parent and is not managed as a persistent fleet resource.</dd></div>
   502	          <div><dt>Agent template</dt><dd>A markdown definition under <code>.claude/agents/</code> whose role, prompt, declared model, and declared tools can seed a primary agent.</dd></div>
   503	          <div><dt>Core four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools—the lens for reading every agent in the fleet.</dd></div>
   504	          <div><dt>R&amp;D</dt><dd>Reduce context and Delegate work; the discipline that keeps the coordinator smaller than the fleet’s combined execution.</dd></div>
   505	          <div><dt>Agent observability</dt><dd>Structured evidence about status, model, context, cost, responses, tools, thinking, hooks, consumed files, produced files, and results.</dd></div>
   506	          <div><dt>Consumed versus Produced</dt><dd>The file activity split between sources an agent read and artifacts it wrote or edited.</dd></div>
   507	          <div><dt>PITER</dt><dd>Prompt input → Trigger → Environment → Review, the out-loop system mapped onto the course app. “Peter” is the transcript artifact.</dd></div>
   508	          <div><dt>Sleep-check loop</dt><dd>Prompt-driven monitoring that alternates Bash sleep with <code>check_agent_status</code> until completion evidence appears.</dd></div>
   509	          <div><dt>Orchestration prompt</dt><dd>A reusable command that instructs the O-agent how to create, command, monitor, connect, report, and retire a multi-agent sequence.</dd></div>
   510	          <div><dt>ADW</dt><dd>AI Developer Workflow; the demonstration composes an ADW-shaped plan → build → review → report sequence, while broader deterministic integration remains future work.</dd></div>
   511	          <div><dt>Results-oriented engineering</dt><dd>The requirement that every agent leave an inspectable, hand-off-ready, durable result.</dd></div>
   512	          <div><dt>Templating your engineering</dt><dd>Encoding your domain structures, prompts, tools, agents, report formats, and review criteria into reusable assets.</dd></div>
   513	          <div><dt>Soft archive</dt><dd>The repository’s implementation of <code>delete_agent</code>: the agent becomes inactive through <code>archived = true</code>, while its row and reserved name remain.</dd></div>
   514	          <div><dt>Agentic layer</dt><dd>The system that builds the system. The O-agent is part of it and helps organize its agents, workflows, and review surfaces.</dd></div>
   515	          <div><dt>Operate in the gray</dt><dd>Use the valuable current system while honestly accounting for cost, maintenance, drift, and missing deterministic/human control features.</dd></div>
   516	        </dl>
   517	      </section>
   518	
   519	      <footer class="chapter-footer">
   520	        <p class="eyebrow">Volume II · Agentic Horizon</p>
