181:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — template pulled up a level</p><h3>A reusable definition becomes a persistent worker</h3><p>The demonstration calls <code>create_agent(subagent_template="scout-report-suggest-fast")</code>. The loader resolves a file under <code>.claude/agents/</code>, uses its prompt and model to mount a fresh client, and stores the session. Later, <code>command_agent</code> resumes that session. The template came from the sub-agent ecosystem, but the runtime gives it primary-agent lifecycle.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/ah-4-multi-agent-orchestration-the-o-agent/t1445-ecosystem-interop-slash-commands.jpg" alt="Orchestrator interface showing a managed codebase-questioner agent running the question slash command and a prime command being typed" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">24:05</span>The managed <code>codebase-questioner</code> runs <code>/question</code>; the answer confirms that orchestrator and agents can use skills, while <code>/prime</code> is ready in the single input.</figcaption></figure></div>
209:        <h2 class="section-title">PITER keeps you out of the loop without abandoning review</h2>
214:        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p><h3>PITER, tactic four</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Transcript correction</p><p><strong>PITER</strong> means Prompt input → Trigger → Environment → Review. The automatic transcript writes “Peter.” PITER is a lesson concept mapped onto the repo; it is not a string to search for in source.</p></aside><p>The associated tactic is Stay Out the Loop. “Review” keeps the phrase honest: the human changes position from continuous relay operator to evaluator at meaningful checkpoints.</p></div>
215:        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — map one request</p><h3>Architecture summary through PITER</h3><div class="result-chain"><article><h3>Out-loop execution</h3><p><strong>P:</strong> request frontend/backend/full-stack summaries.</p><p><strong>T:</strong> one chat request launches O-agent execution.</p><p><strong>E:</strong> three primary agents read the course app directory.</p></article><article><h3>Human review</h3><p><strong>R:</strong> inspect each agent’s status, consumed sources, produced architecture markdown, cost, and diff. Enter the file only when judgment is useful.</p><p>The system carries transitions; the human still decides whether the architecture account is correct.</p></article></div></div>
216:        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — map your environment</p><h3>Make every PITER stage concrete</h3><p>Choose one orchestration-shaped task. Write the exact input, the event or endpoint that triggers it, the directory and permissions that define its environment, and the evidence required for review. If your R stage says only “read the answer,” add a diff, test, artifact, cost, or independent reviewer.</p></div>
218:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>This reference implementation is one PITER mapping</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Transfer without copying the stack</p><p>FastAPI, asyncio, Postgres, WebSockets, and Pinia are the course implementation. A cron trigger, issue event, CLI invocation, or job queue can occupy T; a worktree or container can define E; CI and a review artifact can define R. Preserve the four responsibilities.</p></aside></div>
219:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><div class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Expand PITER.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompt input → Trigger → Environment → Review.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which tactic does it operationalize?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tactic four: Stay Out the Loop.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is “Peter” absent from the repo?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is a transcript artifact for the conceptual acronym PITER, not a code identifier.</p></details></div></div></div>
220:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach PITER using the course app.</strong> Do not use generic definitions; name the request, endpoint, directory, and review evidence.</p><p class="fine">Pass if Review is concrete enough to reject a bad result.</p></div></div>
267:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — existing assets plug into the fleet</p><h3>One coordinator, many reusable surfaces</h3><div class="pillar-grid"><article><strong>Agent templates</strong><p>Seed scout, builder, reviewer, browser validator, docs, and meta-agent roles.</p></article><article><strong>Commands + skills</strong><p>Managed agents can run reusable <code>/question</code>, <code>/prime</code>, and model-invocable workflows.</p></article><article><strong>Hooks + streams</strong><p>Observe lifecycle and file activity; apply the same governance and event patterns.</p></article></div></div>
